.TH "TDDA TAG" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda tag manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda tag\fR — tag tests that failed in the last reference test run

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda tag\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda tag\fR command reads the log of failing tests written by the
most recent logged \fBtdda.referencetest\fR run and adds \fB@tag\fR decorators
to those tests in their source files. Tagged tests can then be run in
isolation, allowing a rapid edit-test cycle focused on failing
tests. A logged run of \fBtdda.referencetest\fR uses \fB--log-failures\fR or
(for unittest-style tests only) \fB-F\fR\&.

.SH WORKFLOW
A typical workflow with \fBunittest\fR-style tests (\fBReferenceTestCase\fR) is:

.PP
\fBpython tests.py -9      # Remove any existing @tag decorators\fR
.br
\fBpython tests.py -F      # Run tests, logging failures\fR
.br
\fBtdda tag                # Add @tag to failing tests\fR
.br
\fBpython tests.py -1      # Run only tagged (failing) tests\fR

.PP
When all tests are passing:

.PP
\fBpython tests.py -9      # Remove @tag decorators\fR

.PP
The equivalent workflow with \fBpytest\fR is:

.PP
\fBpytest --untag          # Remove any existing @tag decorators\fR
.br
\fBpytest --log-failures   # Run tests, logging failures\fR
.br
\fBtdda tag                # Add @tag to failing tests\fR
.br
\fBpytest --tagged         # Run only tagged (failing) tests\fR

.PP
When all tests are passing:

.PP
\fBpytest --untag          # Remove @tag decorators\fR

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtdda(1)\fR
